Phase 3 — Execution Checklist
Purpose

This checklist exists to prevent sloppy execution, uncontrolled drift, and silent disaster.
If you cannot check these boxes honestly, you are not in disciplined execution — you are gambling.

Every stage must be explicitly acknowledged as PASS / FAIL.

1️⃣ Pre-Execution Confirmation

Before ANY hands-on work begins, confirm:

☐ Phase 0 purpose is still valid

☐ Phase 1 framing and scope constraints are still accepted

☐ Phase 2 risks, limits, and boundary conditions are understood and acknowledged

☐ Phase 3 Execution License exists and is valid

☐ Funding exists, not “promised”

☐ Executive sponsor is identified and active

☐ Lead Engineer / Architecture Owner is identified

☐ Independent reviewer is identified

☐ Kill conditions are understood

☐ Rollback authority is defined

If any of these are missing, execution stops. No improvisation.

2️⃣ Alignment & Authority Lock

Execution authority must be explicit. Confirm:

☐ Who can approve changes is documented

☐ Who cannot approve changes is documented

☐ Change review process exists and is written

☐ Concept Drift Control file has been read by the execution team

☐ Everyone understands Phase 3 is execution, not redesign

If roles are fuzzy, the program will fall apart. If authority is unclear, stop.

3️⃣ Requirements Honesty Check

Confirm the system being executed is the same system defined earlier:

☐ Purpose hasn’t “subtly shifted”

☐ Scope hasn’t “expanded just a little”

☐ Constraints haven’t magically “relaxed”

☐ Success isn’t being redefined midstream

☐ Institutional expectations match reality

If someone is already softening reality before hardware exists, that’s drift. Fix it or halt.

4️⃣ Technical Readiness to Execute

Before building or testing:

☐ Minimum viable technical plan exists

☐ Execution steps are sequential and coherent

☐ Dependencies are realistic

☐ Operational environment is honestly defined

☐ Logistics exist, not fantasy

☐ Failure detection plan exists

☐ Rollback path is physically possible

☐ Test success criteria exist

☐ Test failure handling exists

If the plan only works when everything goes perfectly, it is not a plan.

5️⃣ Institutional Stability Check

Execution does not happen in a vacuum. Confirm:

☐ Organization actually wants this done

☐ Political stability around the program exists

☐ No silent expectation of magic outcomes

☐ Leadership understands risk and supports it anyway

☐ Program continuation does not depend on luck or heroic effort

If institutional reality is rotten, execution is just an expensive performance art piece.

6️⃣ Execution Discipline in Motion

During execution:

☐ Changes are documented, not whispered

☐ Drift violations are caught and corrected

☐ Failures are logged honestly

☐ Nobody is allowed to “wing it”

☐ Independent reviewer remains active

☐ Transparency is enforced

If engineering silence replaces engineering honesty — stop.

7️⃣ Rolling Evaluation

Execution is not linear; reality pushes back. Continue only if:

☐ Value proposition still survives

☐ Performance is within tolerances

☐ Risks remain controllable

☐ Execution is still aligned to architecture intent

☐ No cliff-edge compromises have been normalized

If survival depends on pretending everything is fine, that’s failure.

8️⃣ Kill Conditions — Hard Stop Enforcement

If any of these occur, execution halts. Not “discussed.” Halts.

☐ Performance collapses below value threshold

☐ Phase 2 “this would be fatal” risks actually happen

☐ Core assumptions prove false

☐ Execution now requires perfection

☐ Institutional support collapses

☐ Architecture would need to be rewritten to continue

Continuing after this point is ego and self-harm.

9️⃣ Success Conditions

Execution is only “successful” if:

☐ System behavior matches architectural intent

☐ Performance aligns with honest expectations

☐ Execution remained disciplined

☐ Constraints and risks were respected

☐ No silent compromises were buried

☐ The result is something you can defend in public without lying

If you built “something” but it isn’t what Phase 0–2 defined, that’s not success. That’s drift wearing a costume.

10️⃣ Final Integrity Check

Before declaring Phase 3 complete:

☐ Engineers agree

☐ Managers agree

☐ Executives agree

☐ Independent reviewer agrees

☐ No one is quietly swallowing objections

If approval requires politeness, politics, or fear — don’t sign off.
